@meta
id: 20-11
title: Walk the history
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-named
par: 59
minutes: 6
vscode: undo until it reappears, copy it, redo everything, paste
open: registry.ts

@brief
You will not always know which numbered register holds the line you
want. Counting deletes backwards is error-prone, and `:reg` is slow to
read when the lines look alike. Vim has a trick for exactly this: after
`"1p`, the dot command puts the *next* register. `"1p` `u` `.` `u` `.`
walks down the history one delete at a time, undoing each wrong guess,
until the right line appears.

Four registrations in this registry are for classes that no longer
exist: `LegacyRefundProvider`, `EmailService`, `InvoiceRepository` and
`AuditProvider`. Delete all four, in file order. Then it turns out
`EmailService` was not removed — it moved to infrastructure. Its
registration goes back, at the bottom of the file, from the history.
Find it by walking, not by counting.

@keys
| `"1p` | put the most recent delete |
| `u` `.` | undo it, and put the next one down instead |
| `:reg 1234` | the other way to find it |

@hints
Delete the four lines with `dd`, using search and `n` to get from one
to the next rather than reading line numbers.
--
`G` to the bottom, then `"1p`. Wrong line? `u` then `.` — the dot
increments the register for you. Keep going until `EmailService`
lands.
--
`/Legacy` Enter `dd`, `/Email` Enter `dd`, `/Invoice` Enter `dd`,
`/Audit` Enter `dd`, `G`, `"1p`, `u.`, `u.`.

@solution
    /Legacy<Enter> dd /Email<Enter> dd /Invoice<Enter> dd /Audit<Enter> dd
    G "1p u. u.  :wq<Enter>

`EmailService` was the second of four deletes, so it sits in `"3`, and
`"3p` gets it directly if you counted right. The walk is for when you
did not count, which is most of the time: `"1p` shows you `"1`, and each
`u.` after that shows the next one down. Three deletes ago is never more
than three steps away.

This is the one place the dot command changes what it repeats. Normally
`.` replays the last change exactly; after a put from a numbered
register it bumps the number first. It is a deliberate special case in
vim, and it exists because walking the history by hand — `"1p`, `u`,
`"2p`, `u`, `"3p` — is fiddly enough that people did not do it.

@start registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('LegacyRefundProvider', { useClass: LegacyRefundProvider });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('EmailService', { useClass: EmailService });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('InvoiceRepository', { useClass: InvoiceRepository });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('AuditProvider', { useClass: AuditProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });

@expected registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
container.register('EmailService', { useClass: EmailService });
